1
00:00:01,417 --> 00:00:03,044
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:03,169 --> 00:00:04,962
Yeah. Lena's on a short list
for a position

3
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:06,339
in the Commerce Department in D.C.

4
00:00:06,464 --> 00:00:07,507
It's not a done deal yet.

5
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:09,175
I have to be confirmed,
and that could take months.

6
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:11,844
This is Bill Peterson,
San Antonio DEA.

7
00:00:11,969 --> 00:00:13,554
What does this guy Peterson
have on you?

8
00:00:13,763 --> 00:00:16,891
When I was your boss at Rio Bravo
I had your back.

9
00:00:17,016 --> 00:00:18,726
You telling me
you don't have mine now?

10
00:00:18,893 --> 00:00:21,020
Jane, I'm on the 6th floor now.

11
00:00:21,145 --> 00:00:23,898
Lisbon, it looks like he may be
at the other end. Keep going.

12
00:00:24,023 --> 00:00:27,193
You took me off on purpose.
You have to let me do my job.

13
00:00:27,318 --> 00:00:29,695
If you can't, how do we work together?

14
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,073
Peterson can end my career.
I can go to prison.

15
00:00:32,323 --> 00:00:36,744
Any mention of this could end things
for Lena before it even starts.

16
00:00:36,869 --> 00:00:38,120
Well, you can't let him do it.

17
00:00:38,246 --> 00:00:41,415
It's over for you. And your wife.
I'm gonna see to that.

18
00:01:45,062 --> 00:01:47,023
{\an8}"Everything turned out
exactly as we planned."

19
00:01:47,148 --> 00:01:49,108
- I know.
- No one was hurt.

20
00:01:49,317 --> 00:01:51,569
- I know.
- Well, except the bad guy, of course

21
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:53,529
but he was an assassin.

22
00:01:53,738 --> 00:01:55,573
I understand. Mission accomplished.

23
00:01:55,698 --> 00:01:57,992
But in the middle of our mission
you directed me to go somewhere

24
00:01:58,117 --> 00:01:59,535
that had nothing to do
with the mission.

25
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:01,162
Yeah, I was concerned
about your safety.

26
00:02:01,329 --> 00:02:03,205
- You don't get it.
- I don't get what?

27
00:02:03,372 --> 00:02:06,042
- I'm an FBI agent.
- Uh, I think I do get that.

28
00:02:06,167 --> 00:02:08,085
- No, I don't think you do.
- Excuse me.

29
00:02:09,795 --> 00:02:10,838
Wiley?

30
00:02:11,172 --> 00:02:12,590
You asked me to keep tabs on Peterson

31
00:02:12,715 --> 00:02:14,383
let you know if he's making
any suspicious moves.

32
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:16,927
- "Yeah. What's he done?"
- According to his credit card record

33
00:02:17,053 --> 00:02:20,264
two days ago he bought a plane ticket
to Mexico. He flew there this morning.

34
00:02:20,389 --> 00:02:21,932
Well, he might have gone
down there for work.

35
00:02:22,558 --> 00:02:24,894
I checked. His office said
he's at a conference in St. Paul.

36
00:02:25,019 --> 00:02:28,522
Okay, well, could you look
into Peterson's phone records for us?

37
00:02:29,065 --> 00:02:30,274
We'll be right in.

38
00:02:30,483 --> 00:02:32,818
- What am I looking for?
- Well, something fishy.

39
00:02:32,985 --> 00:02:34,987
"Fishy. Got it."

40
00:02:35,404 --> 00:02:36,530
Is that about Abbott?

41
00:02:36,697 --> 00:02:38,532
- Sounds like it.
- Well, what are we gonna do?

42
00:02:39,575 --> 00:02:40,701
Whatever we can.

43
00:02:40,868 --> 00:02:43,871
Peterson's checked into a hotel
just outside Rio Bravo.

44
00:02:44,246 --> 00:02:45,456
That's where he worked
with Abbott, right?

45
00:02:45,581 --> 00:02:48,376
- Yeah, ten years ago.
- I checked his phone record.

46
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:50,127
The only call that stuck out

47
00:02:50,252 --> 00:02:52,672
was to a San Antonio Congressman
this afternoon--

48
00:02:52,797 --> 00:02:54,465
representative Jules Blatt.

49
00:02:54,799 --> 00:02:55,883
I've heard of him.

50
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:58,260
He's a nobody trying to make
a name for himself.

51
00:02:58,386 --> 00:03:00,388
Well, if he could block
Lena Abbott's confirmation

52
00:03:00,513 --> 00:03:03,015
it would buff his reputation
in certain circles, wouldn't it?

53
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:05,559
How would Peterson going
to Rio Bravo help that?

54
00:03:05,726 --> 00:03:07,812
I don't know.
The hearings start in four days

55
00:03:07,937 --> 00:03:11,023
so if Peterson wants to make trouble
now is the time to do it.

56
00:03:11,232 --> 00:03:12,400
Well, what are we gonna do?

57
00:03:13,359 --> 00:03:14,777
What are we gonna do
about what?

58
00:03:17,822 --> 00:03:19,365
Why is everyone whispering?

59
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:23,619
How do you know about all this?

60
00:03:24,537 --> 00:03:26,455
Wylie's keeping tabs on Peterson.

61
00:03:27,164 --> 00:03:28,499
And you brought Wylie into it?

62
00:03:28,791 --> 00:03:32,044
Well, I mentioned that Peterson
might be making some trouble.

63
00:03:32,169 --> 00:03:35,256
He volunteered. The kid
wouldn't take no for an answer.

64
00:03:35,631 --> 00:03:37,925
Do you have any idea
what Peterson is doing in Rio Bravo?

65
00:03:39,927 --> 00:03:41,262
Peterson's careful.

66
00:03:41,637 --> 00:03:43,222
He wouldn't make any accusations

67
00:03:43,347 --> 00:03:45,683
if he didn't have the evidence
to back it up.

68
00:03:46,892 --> 00:03:48,102
I shot a man.

69
00:03:48,561 --> 00:03:49,812
Bullet's in the body.

70
00:03:51,313 --> 00:03:53,899
Peterson found the corpse,
dug out the bullet

71
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,446
all he's gotta do is subpoena the gun
and... that's game over.

72
00:04:00,614 --> 00:04:01,824
I need to call Lena.

73
00:04:04,827 --> 00:04:06,454
I'll let you do that.

74
00:04:11,625 --> 00:04:13,377
What does he think Peterson
has on him?

75
00:04:13,502 --> 00:04:15,921
- The bullet.
- Well, if he does, then Abbott's toast.

76
00:04:16,046 --> 00:04:17,256
That's a little pessimistic.

77
00:04:17,381 --> 00:04:19,300
But this isn't just
about Lena's confirmation.

78
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:21,719
Abbott will lose his job.
He could go to prison.

79
00:04:21,844 --> 00:04:23,095
I don't think that's gonna happen.

80
00:04:23,429 --> 00:04:25,181
- Why not?
- We'll prevent it.

81
00:04:25,431 --> 00:04:26,807
In four days? How?

82
00:04:27,308 --> 00:04:28,601
I'm not quite sure yet.

83
00:04:30,144 --> 00:04:32,104
Jane, this is serious.

84
00:04:32,229 --> 00:04:35,024
I know. Don't you love it
when the stakes are high?

85
00:04:49,253 --> 00:04:50,462
I came as soon as I could.

86
00:04:51,463 --> 00:04:52,756
Thanks for coming, honey.

87
00:04:55,509 --> 00:04:56,635
Let's talk in my office.

88
00:04:59,304 --> 00:05:00,347
Peterson?

89
00:05:01,515 --> 00:05:02,558
Yes.

90
00:05:02,891 --> 00:05:05,227
{\an8}- You're sure he has the evidence?
- Sure enough.

91
00:05:05,686 --> 00:05:07,521
{\an8}Look, baby, I am so sorry.

92
00:05:07,646 --> 00:05:09,106
{\an8}I should've told you everything
from the start.

93
00:05:09,231 --> 00:05:10,732
- Don't say that.
- No, no, no, it's my fault.

94
00:05:11,233 --> 00:05:13,277
{\an8}- I really let you down.
- Do not say that.

95
00:05:14,736 --> 00:05:16,613
{\an8}You don't have to apologize, not to me.

96
00:05:17,197 --> 00:05:18,448
Not to anyone.

97
00:05:18,949 --> 00:05:20,784
{\an8}That this person
would try to bring you down

98
00:05:20,909 --> 00:05:22,452
{\an8}makes me so angry.

99
00:05:24,037 --> 00:05:26,707
- This is simple. I'll withdraw my name.
- No.

100
00:05:26,832 --> 00:05:29,251
I don't want the job
if this is what it takes to get it.

101
00:05:29,376 --> 00:05:32,379
{\an8}Look, I'm not gonna have you quit
because of me. Okay?

102
00:05:32,504 --> 00:05:34,631
Now, I-I'll tell Peterson I'll resign.

103
00:05:34,756 --> 00:05:38,635
{\an8}- Absolutely not.
- We can just go along with the same story

104
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:41,889
{\an8}- that we're separated, and maybe--
- Excuse me?

105
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:43,682
Jane, could you please
just give us a minute, please?

106
00:05:43,807 --> 00:05:45,100
{\an8}Yeah, I would, but time is short

107
00:05:45,225 --> 00:05:47,477
{\an8}and I'm guessing that each of you
is offering to fall on your sword

108
00:05:47,644 --> 00:05:49,688
{\an8}for the other, which is very
loving and admirable

109
00:05:49,813 --> 00:05:51,982
{\an8}- but I don't think it'll work.
- Why not?

110
00:05:52,107 --> 00:05:54,401
{\an8}Because Peterson is ruthless
and determined

111
00:05:54,526 --> 00:05:56,987
{\an8}and he won't stop
until he's destroyed both of you.

112
00:05:57,112 --> 00:05:59,656
{\an8}Now we knew he was gonna make
a move like this sooner or later.

113
00:05:59,907 --> 00:06:02,993
{\an8}- Now it is time for us to make our move.
- Which is what, exactly?

114
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:05,203
{\an8}The hearings are in three days.

115
00:06:05,412 --> 00:06:07,456
{\an8}Well, I think I can come up
with something if...

116
00:06:07,915 --> 00:06:09,541
you both want me involved.

117
00:06:10,167 --> 00:06:12,210
{\an8}No, no, this is--
this is not your problem.

118
00:06:12,336 --> 00:06:14,671
{\an8}Wait a minute. Dennis tells me
you have one of the trickiest

119
00:06:14,796 --> 00:06:16,381
{\an8}and most devious minds he's worked with.

120
00:06:16,548 --> 00:06:18,050
He did, did he? Ah.

121
00:06:18,175 --> 00:06:20,552
Well, I didn't come here to be
flattered, but, please, go on.

122
00:06:20,802 --> 00:06:22,804
He also says you're his friend

123
00:06:22,971 --> 00:06:24,264
and that he would trust you
with his life.

124
00:06:26,141 --> 00:06:28,518
- Can you really help?
- I think so.

125
00:06:31,229 --> 00:06:32,314
{\an8}Dennis.

126
00:06:34,399 --> 00:06:35,859
{\an8}What do you have in mind?

127
00:06:37,069 --> 00:06:38,654
I can't tell you.

128
00:06:38,779 --> 00:06:40,530
We need your hands to be clean.

129
00:06:40,989 --> 00:06:42,824
Deniability and such.

130
00:06:50,248 --> 00:06:51,792
Okay, we'll do it your way.

131
00:06:53,210 --> 00:06:54,252
Excellent.

132
00:06:57,339 --> 00:06:58,882
Hey, any updates?

133
00:06:59,007 --> 00:07:00,842
Peterson arrived back
in San Antonio two hours ago.

134
00:07:00,968 --> 00:07:02,970
Has he had any more contact
with Congressman Blatt?

135
00:07:03,095 --> 00:07:04,471
No, it's just the one
phone call yesterday.

136
00:07:04,596 --> 00:07:06,682
It lasted 30 seconds. It was probably
just a voice mail message.

137
00:07:06,807 --> 00:07:07,849
I hear Abbott's in trouble.

138
00:07:09,518 --> 00:07:10,936
I'm not very good
at keeping secrets.

139
00:07:11,061 --> 00:07:12,354
Good thing you're in the FBI

140
00:07:12,562 --> 00:07:15,107
{\an8}- What are we doing about it?
- Jane's coming up with a plan right now.

141
00:07:15,482 --> 00:07:17,859
{\an8}You might wanna think twice
before getting in on this one.

142
00:07:17,985 --> 00:07:19,027
{\an8}Both of you.

143
00:07:19,236 --> 00:07:21,363
{\an8}I have a feeling we're gonna be
pretty far outside the lines.

144
00:07:21,613 --> 00:07:23,073
Well, Jane had me pretend
to kill a guy once.

145
00:07:23,198 --> 00:07:24,616
Are we going further outside
the lines than that?

146
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:26,243
Probably not.

147
00:07:26,743 --> 00:07:27,828
Then I'm good.

148
00:07:28,870 --> 00:07:29,997
Me, too.

149
00:07:30,372 --> 00:07:31,790
Let's take this outta the bullpen.

150
00:07:35,711 --> 00:07:36,878
{\an8}What did Abbott say?

151
00:07:37,129 --> 00:07:39,089
{\an8}- All systems go.
- Great.

152
00:07:40,048 --> 00:07:43,218
- So what do you got, Wylie?
- Bill Peterson is 55 years old.

153
00:07:43,343 --> 00:07:45,637
He's been a DEA agent for 27 years.

154
00:07:45,762 --> 00:07:49,099
Married briefly in his 30s.
His ex-wife lives in Tacoma.

155
00:07:49,224 --> 00:07:50,684
No kids, no living relatives.

156
00:07:50,809 --> 00:07:52,936
- What does he like doing?
- Work, mostly.

157
00:07:53,103 --> 00:07:55,480
Plays a little tennis,
goes scuba-diving in the Caribbean.

158
00:07:56,023 --> 00:07:59,443
Based on what he buys at the local
liquor store, he's a wine snob.

159
00:08:00,235 --> 00:08:01,611
- Interesting.
- Why?

160
00:08:01,737 --> 00:08:04,031
Obviously he's as corrupt
as the day is long.

161
00:08:04,156 --> 00:08:05,866
- Why obviously?
- Well, a man that likes tennis

162
00:08:05,991 --> 00:08:09,036
travel, and a good Bordeaux
isn't retiring on a government pension.

163
00:08:09,161 --> 00:08:11,079
He has some loot stashed someplace.

164
00:08:11,246 --> 00:08:12,289
We need to find it.

165
00:08:12,414 --> 00:08:14,207
What about the evidence
Peterson found in Mexico?

166
00:08:14,374 --> 00:08:15,417
The bullet--
what are we gonna do about that?

167
00:08:15,542 --> 00:08:17,419
I'll take care of the bullet.
You dig down on Peterson.

168
00:08:17,544 --> 00:08:19,296
He's been a DEA agent for 30 years.

169
00:08:19,421 --> 00:08:20,756
There's a ton of ways
he could be stealing.

170
00:08:21,089 --> 00:08:23,675
He could be taking payoffs from a cartel
he could be selling the drugs himself.

171
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:25,260
Yeah, look into stuff like that.

172
00:08:25,719 --> 00:08:28,472
I have to go make some calls
and catch up with an old friend.

173
00:08:46,239 --> 00:08:47,365
- Hi.
- We catch a case?

174
00:08:48,533 --> 00:08:50,368
- No.
- What are you all working on?

175
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:51,703
Nothing.

176
00:08:51,953 --> 00:08:54,039
Sure looks like something.
Anything I can do to help?

177
00:08:54,164 --> 00:08:56,583
Yeah.
You can hold down the fort out here.

178
00:08:58,085 --> 00:09:01,171
- Did I do something wrong?
- No. Just stay out here, okay?

179
00:09:12,099 --> 00:09:13,308
Mr. Barsocky.

180
00:09:14,434 --> 00:09:16,812
You crazy circus freak.

181
00:09:17,354 --> 00:09:19,397
Patrick Jane, you son of a bitch.

182
00:09:19,523 --> 00:09:21,149
Looking good, Pete. Very relaxed.

183
00:09:21,316 --> 00:09:25,821
Well, I'm as a fat and wrinkled
as Daisy the amazing elephant, but thanks.

184
00:09:26,279 --> 00:09:29,157
And thanks for paving the way to Austin.

185
00:09:29,407 --> 00:09:30,909
First class tickets and everything.

186
00:09:31,201 --> 00:09:33,328
You should see that hotel room
you paid for.

187
00:09:33,495 --> 00:09:35,789
Well, that is my pleasure, Pete.
How's things?

188
00:09:36,665 --> 00:09:40,335
You know. Looking for rubes to fleece.
You?

189
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:42,963
I'm-I'm seeing someone
at the moment, actually.

190
00:09:43,630 --> 00:09:44,840
You dog.

191
00:09:44,965 --> 00:09:47,300
Wait, is it that pretty brunette
that came around with you

192
00:09:47,467 --> 00:09:48,802
a couple years ago--
the cop?

193
00:09:48,927 --> 00:09:51,179
- As a matter of fact, it is.
- I knew it.

194
00:09:51,346 --> 00:09:52,472
I could tell she was sweet on you.

195
00:09:52,597 --> 00:09:54,432
Well, she's a little cranky
with me right now.

196
00:09:55,058 --> 00:09:56,643
Women. Who gets 'em?

197
00:09:58,812 --> 00:10:00,188
But I'm happy to hear it.

198
00:10:02,023 --> 00:10:04,818
You should have someone, Patrick.
You deserve to be happy.

199
00:10:04,985 --> 00:10:06,027
Thank you.

200
00:10:07,362 --> 00:10:08,989
Maybe it's time, you know?

201
00:10:10,115 --> 00:10:11,491
Take the ring off.

202
00:10:12,033 --> 00:10:14,744
No shame in moving on.
Angela would want you to.

203
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:17,330
Yeah. Thanks, Pete.
I need a favor.

204
00:10:17,455 --> 00:10:20,000
I have a friend who's in a little trouble
with the law

205
00:10:20,125 --> 00:10:21,293
and I want to get him out of it.

206
00:10:21,418 --> 00:10:23,378
- Sounds like fun to me.
- That's what I thought.

207
00:10:23,753 --> 00:10:25,839
So, here's the gag...

208
00:10:25,964 --> 00:10:27,924
That's all fine, but I need
those other files.

209
00:10:29,509 --> 00:10:32,345
Yeah, get me the--
the second quarter arrest figures

210
00:10:32,679 --> 00:10:34,598
on my desk in the morning, all right?

211
00:10:35,098 --> 00:10:37,058
- Yeah. See you then.
- Sir? Excuse me?

212
00:10:37,184 --> 00:10:39,102
This is a private club. You can't--
Sir?

213
00:10:40,645 --> 00:10:41,897
- Sir?
- Who let you in?

214
00:10:42,022 --> 00:10:44,316
Oh, I'm just taking a little look around
thinking about membership.

215
00:10:44,482 --> 00:10:45,650
Is that supposed to be amusing?

216
00:10:45,775 --> 00:10:48,028
Mr. Peterson, this man said
that he was from your office.

217
00:10:48,195 --> 00:10:49,362
Is there a problem?

218
00:10:49,654 --> 00:10:50,989
It's fine, Stacey.

219
00:10:51,114 --> 00:10:54,075
Stacey, Mr. Peterson and I go
way back. Thank you.

220
00:10:55,785 --> 00:10:57,662
How was your little trip down
to Rio Bravo?

221
00:10:58,371 --> 00:10:59,998
- You know about that?
- Of course I do.

222
00:11:00,165 --> 00:11:02,918
You think I'm gonna sit back
and watch you destroy Dennis Abbott?

223
00:11:03,043 --> 00:11:04,711
I'm not trying to destroy anyone.

224
00:11:05,212 --> 00:11:06,504
I'm after justice.

225
00:11:07,547 --> 00:11:09,674
Dennis Abbott murdered
a man in cold blood.

226
00:11:09,841 --> 00:11:13,053
A commander of one of the largest
drug cartels in Mexico.

227
00:11:13,345 --> 00:11:14,638
Murdered him nonetheless.

228
00:11:14,763 --> 00:11:17,265
Don't you have better things
to do, Peterson?

229
00:11:17,390 --> 00:11:19,643
Like update your tennis gear?
What is this?

230
00:11:20,435 --> 00:11:21,645
Oh, it's velour.

231
00:11:24,189 --> 00:11:25,982
Let me tell you something, Jane.

232
00:11:26,149 --> 00:11:28,652
I don't give a rat's ass
about Dennis Abbott.

233
00:11:29,319 --> 00:11:31,655
- I see an opportunity.
- How do you figure?

234
00:11:33,073 --> 00:11:35,367
There's a new wave coming into D.C.

235
00:11:35,492 --> 00:11:38,495
If I can help a Congressman
do something opportune, uh--

236
00:11:38,703 --> 00:11:42,582
block an appointment
that his party is desperate to kill, say--

237
00:11:42,749 --> 00:11:44,084
he's in my debt.

238
00:11:44,459 --> 00:11:47,003
It's money in the bank.
The future, Jane.

239
00:11:47,337 --> 00:11:48,755
That's what I'm thinking about.

240
00:11:49,339 --> 00:11:52,509
Screwing Dennis Abbott--
that's just icing on the cake.

241
00:11:52,634 --> 00:11:53,718
It won't work.

242
00:11:54,344 --> 00:11:56,137
- We'll see.
- Yes, we will.

243
00:11:57,347 --> 00:11:58,765
We shall see.

244
00:12:00,183 --> 00:12:04,104
I'm really sorry, Mr. Peterson.
He was very persuasive.

245
00:12:06,022 --> 00:12:07,065
Stacey...

246
00:12:07,983 --> 00:12:09,359
Have you seen my cell phone?

247
00:12:09,859 --> 00:12:11,528
Um... No.

248
00:12:50,397 --> 00:12:51,440
Morning.

249
00:12:52,751 --> 00:12:53,794
Here.

250
00:12:54,485 --> 00:12:55,528
Thanks.

251
00:12:55,653 --> 00:12:56,987
Did you get anything off the phone?

252
00:12:57,988 --> 00:12:59,657
I pulled all of Peterson's contacts

253
00:12:59,782 --> 00:13:01,700
and cross-referenced them
with our database.

254
00:13:01,826 --> 00:13:04,912
I checked his call history,
his e-mails, his texts...

255
00:13:05,037 --> 00:13:06,288
- And?
- Nothing.

256
00:13:06,497 --> 00:13:08,958
Nothing that looks like contact
with a cartel member.

257
00:13:09,083 --> 00:13:11,126
If Peterson's getting money
under the table

258
00:13:11,252 --> 00:13:12,837
I don't know who's giving it to him.

259
00:13:12,962 --> 00:13:14,421
Cho and I couldn't find anything, either.

260
00:13:14,588 --> 00:13:17,174
We looked into offshore corporations
third parties' accounts.

261
00:13:17,341 --> 00:13:19,677
There's no sign that money's
being hidden or laundered.

262
00:13:19,802 --> 00:13:22,346
Well, of course not. He knows
that's the first place someone would look.

263
00:13:22,471 --> 00:13:23,806
So where is he putting the money?

264
00:13:24,265 --> 00:13:26,475
Got me. Nothing about him
really sticks out

265
00:13:26,642 --> 00:13:28,227
except that he's super diligent.

266
00:13:28,352 --> 00:13:29,895
Works 60 hours a week or more

267
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:32,314
and two to three times a month
he goes out with the teams on raids.

268
00:13:32,439 --> 00:13:33,482
What kinda raids?

269
00:13:33,607 --> 00:13:37,069
E-mails mention low-level busts--
neighborhood labs, local dealers.

270
00:13:37,236 --> 00:13:39,113
Low-level busts?
That doesn't sound like Peterson.

271
00:13:39,238 --> 00:13:42,783
Yeah, he wouldn't get his hands dirty
unless there was something in it for him.

272
00:13:42,908 --> 00:13:45,452
- I'll bet he's skimming.
- By George, I think she's got it.

273
00:13:45,578 --> 00:13:47,997
- Skimming what?
- When a raid goes down

274
00:13:48,122 --> 00:13:49,874
the DEA agent arrests the dealers

275
00:13:50,207 --> 00:13:52,042
"and then they process
the cash and the drugs"

276
00:13:52,209 --> 00:13:53,419
"that have been seized."

277
00:13:54,044 --> 00:13:57,381
"The senior agent is supposed to record
the amount of money and drugs"

278
00:13:57,506 --> 00:13:59,842
'from the crime scene
before it's put into evidence."

279
00:14:00,634 --> 00:14:02,720
"Unless he's taking some for himself."

280
00:14:03,053 --> 00:14:04,179
"Nobody knows."

281
00:14:04,346 --> 00:14:06,765
If the drug dealer complains,
nobody believes him.

282
00:14:06,891 --> 00:14:08,350
It's kinda the perfect crime.

283
00:14:08,475 --> 00:14:11,478
10,000 here, 50,000 there.
He could have millions by now.

284
00:14:11,604 --> 00:14:13,397
Yeah, but where is he hiding
the money?

285
00:14:13,522 --> 00:14:15,733
Based on the security app
on Peterson's phone

286
00:14:15,858 --> 00:14:19,069
it looks like his house has
a crazy elaborate security system.

287
00:14:19,194 --> 00:14:20,863
Well, there's your answer.
The money's in the house.

288
00:14:20,988 --> 00:14:22,448
If we could get our hands
on that cash

289
00:14:22,573 --> 00:14:24,950
and prove that Peterson's stealing
we'd put an end to this right now.

290
00:14:25,075 --> 00:14:26,660
I don't see how we get in the house.

291
00:14:26,827 --> 00:14:28,954
It's like Fort Knox,
and we only have 48 hours.

292
00:14:29,121 --> 00:14:32,166
Don't be defeatist. Can you pull up
the plan of the security system?

293
00:14:32,708 --> 00:14:33,792
Yeah, I think so.

294
00:14:51,602 --> 00:14:53,687
- Bill Peterson.
- "Hello, Mr. Peterson."

295
00:14:53,812 --> 00:14:57,650
"This is Missy Brammer. I'm Congressman
Blatt's legislative director."

296
00:14:57,775 --> 00:15:00,361
I'm following up
on your call of a few days back.

297
00:15:00,486 --> 00:15:04,907
Uh, as I mentioned, I'd very much like
to speak to Congressman Blatt

298
00:15:05,074 --> 00:15:08,702
about the nomination
of Lena Abbott to the commerce post.

299
00:15:08,869 --> 00:15:11,372
"Yes, the Congressman
was intrigued by your message."

300
00:15:11,497 --> 00:15:12,539
"He'd like to know more."

301
00:15:12,665 --> 00:15:15,417
He's in D.C. right now,
as the hearings are in two days.

302
00:15:15,542 --> 00:15:18,087
"Could you and I meet this evening
to discuss it?"

303
00:15:18,253 --> 00:15:20,255
- Yeah, well, that'd be fine.
- "Good."

304
00:15:20,506 --> 00:15:22,675
8:00 p.m. at the Wagon Wheel
on Alamo.

305
00:15:22,841 --> 00:15:24,510
- "I'll make a reservation."
- See you then.

306
00:15:37,356 --> 00:15:39,858
The good news is Peterson's house
is only one floor

307
00:15:39,984 --> 00:15:41,735
and is easily accessed from the street.

308
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:42,903
Well, what's the bad news?

309
00:15:43,028 --> 00:15:44,697
There are 17 security cameras

310
00:15:44,863 --> 00:15:47,074
six motion sensors, and one cat.

311
00:15:47,241 --> 00:15:49,326
I hate cats. They always try to jump
on me.

312
00:15:49,535 --> 00:15:50,828
Oh, what about the security system?

313
00:15:51,245 --> 00:15:54,081
I downloaded an app from his phone
that monitors the cameras.

314
00:15:54,206 --> 00:15:57,084
I can hack into the forensic software
and get at the root folders.

315
00:15:57,209 --> 00:15:59,169
If he checks in remotely,
it'll look like no one's in the house

316
00:15:59,336 --> 00:16:00,754
but I'll need to do it from here.

317
00:16:06,343 --> 00:16:08,637
- Can you turn off the alarm?
- You'll need to do that at the house.

318
00:16:08,762 --> 00:16:10,014
I can't do it with the phone.

319
00:16:15,144 --> 00:16:17,438
- Hi.
- I'd like to speak to Cho, please.

320
00:16:18,272 --> 00:16:19,314
Sure.

321
00:16:20,858 --> 00:16:21,900
Cho.

322
00:16:26,864 --> 00:16:27,906
What's up?

323
00:16:28,032 --> 00:16:31,076
Something's obviously going on
and I'm here to protest my exclusion.

324
00:16:31,952 --> 00:16:33,829
- Vega.
- Jane is planning something

325
00:16:33,954 --> 00:16:36,248
and I'm pretty sure it has to do
with whatever's going on with Abbott.

326
00:16:36,373 --> 00:16:37,750
- Don't worry about it.
- I do, though.

327
00:16:37,875 --> 00:16:39,877
If Abbott's in trouble, I wanna help.

328
00:16:41,295 --> 00:16:42,379
No.

329
00:16:43,756 --> 00:16:45,591
- You're letting Wylie help.
- Wylie's different.

330
00:16:45,716 --> 00:16:48,469
- I don't feel responsible for him.
- You're not responsible for me.

331
00:16:49,344 --> 00:16:51,722
Vega, if this goes wrong,
it's a career killer.

332
00:16:51,847 --> 00:16:53,891
You'll be finished at the FBI
before you even get started.

333
00:16:55,267 --> 00:16:56,393
I'll take that risk.

334
00:16:58,270 --> 00:17:00,189
What we're discussing involves
breaking about seven laws.

335
00:17:00,314 --> 00:17:01,607
As long as it's only seven.

336
00:17:03,067 --> 00:17:06,195
Either I'm a part of this team,
or I'm not.

337
00:17:08,781 --> 00:17:10,115
We need eyes on Bill Peterson.

338
00:17:10,449 --> 00:17:12,451
Where he's going, what he's doing.

339
00:17:12,576 --> 00:17:14,369
- You think you can do that?
- Absolutely.

340
00:17:14,495 --> 00:17:16,371
- Go.
- Thank you, sir.

341
00:17:19,583 --> 00:17:20,959
Don't call me "sir".

342
00:17:24,421 --> 00:17:25,964
- Vega.
- Sir.

343
00:17:27,132 --> 00:17:28,175
Where are you going?

344
00:17:28,383 --> 00:17:29,426
Just running an errand.

345
00:17:37,851 --> 00:17:39,686
Jane, my office, please.

346
00:17:46,318 --> 00:17:48,695
- Where's Lena?
- I just dropped her off at the airport.

347
00:17:48,821 --> 00:17:51,615
She's headed back to D.C.
What's going on?

348
00:17:52,574 --> 00:17:55,702
Well, like I said, I--
I can't really let you in on it.

349
00:17:56,954 --> 00:18:00,791
Okay, I assume you're breaking
every rule and regulation the FBI has.

350
00:18:00,916 --> 00:18:02,543
Well, that's a little over the top.

351
00:18:03,127 --> 00:18:05,295
Look, I know I said we'd do it
your way

352
00:18:05,420 --> 00:18:06,755
but I-I've changed my mind.

353
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:09,883
I can't have my people taking risks
like this on my behalf.

354
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:12,761
I'll take what comes.

355
00:18:13,262 --> 00:18:14,596
And your wife?

356
00:18:16,390 --> 00:18:18,892
- I think I can keep her out of it.
- You know that's not true.

357
00:18:19,059 --> 00:18:20,978
What you did at Rio Bravo--

358
00:18:21,103 --> 00:18:23,021
you risked your life
to take out a mass murderer

359
00:18:23,188 --> 00:18:24,606
that no one else could...

360
00:18:24,857 --> 00:18:26,692
No one else would.

361
00:18:27,234 --> 00:18:31,280
You did that 'cause you're a good man.
Peterson is not a good man.

362
00:18:31,905 --> 00:18:33,991
He's a greedy, corrupt bureaucrat

363
00:18:34,116 --> 00:18:36,743
with a very healthy sprinkling
of sociopath on top.

364
00:18:36,869 --> 00:18:38,495
- I can handle Peterson.
- No, you won't.

365
00:18:38,620 --> 00:18:40,497
And if we stop now, you're going to prison

366
00:18:40,622 --> 00:18:41,874
your wife's gonna lose her career

367
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:45,169
and Peterson is on his way
to being the head of the DEA.

368
00:18:45,294 --> 00:18:47,462
Is that what you want?
It's not what I want.

369
00:18:49,006 --> 00:18:50,632
And for what it's worth

370
00:18:51,675 --> 00:18:54,511
everyone else feels the same way.

371
00:18:56,471 --> 00:18:57,681
It's gonna be fine.

372
00:18:58,849 --> 00:18:59,892
Chin up.

373
00:19:01,018 --> 00:19:02,060
Trust.

374
00:19:35,135 --> 00:19:36,929
"Obviously, the hearings are this week"

375
00:19:37,095 --> 00:19:39,556
and Dennis Abbott is very much
in our sight.

376
00:19:39,973 --> 00:19:42,517
The issue of what happened
at Rio Bravo

377
00:19:42,643 --> 00:19:46,897
will be key to stopping
Lena Abbott's confirmation.

378
00:19:47,022 --> 00:19:48,774
I'm so glad we're on the same page.

379
00:19:49,816 --> 00:19:52,694
Well, a hearing's a tricky thing.

380
00:19:52,861 --> 00:19:55,697
Really it's about showmanship.
You display it--

381
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:59,451
a little theatrics, if you will.
It is D.C. after all.

382
00:19:59,576 --> 00:20:03,830
As it happens, Ms. Brammer
I have something you can use.

383
00:20:03,956 --> 00:20:06,708
Would you be able to bring it
to the office tomorrow?

384
00:20:07,668 --> 00:20:08,710
I would.

385
00:20:09,169 --> 00:20:10,212
Good.

386
00:20:16,051 --> 00:20:17,886
Cho, it's happening tomorrow.

387
00:20:18,262 --> 00:20:20,514
Whatever you're planning,
we have to do it now.

388
00:20:28,628 --> 00:20:30,338
I'm connected
to Peterson's security system.

389
00:20:31,939 --> 00:20:33,566
Looks like he's getting ready to leave.

390
00:20:56,672 --> 00:20:58,090
Wylie, it's your time.

391
00:20:58,215 --> 00:20:59,258
Just give me a minute.

392
00:21:02,428 --> 00:21:03,679
Okay, we're good to go.

393
00:21:03,804 --> 00:21:06,015
If Peterson checks his cameras,
he'll see a static image

394
00:21:06,140 --> 00:21:07,850
showing no activity
in or outside the house.

395
00:21:08,017 --> 00:21:09,060
Great.

396
00:21:09,185 --> 00:21:10,603
But I can only hold it
for about an hour and a half.

397
00:21:11,145 --> 00:21:12,563
Well, that should give us
enough time.

398
00:21:15,816 --> 00:21:16,984
So what's your plan?

399
00:21:17,485 --> 00:21:20,863
Well... Pick the lock
open the door, see what happens.

400
00:21:20,988 --> 00:21:23,449
- What'll happen is the alarm will go off.
- Not right away.

401
00:21:23,574 --> 00:21:25,576
Alarms give you a minute
to 90-second grace period

402
00:21:25,701 --> 00:21:26,869
before they alert anyone.

403
00:21:27,036 --> 00:21:29,121
That should give me enough time
to figure out the passcode.

404
00:21:29,246 --> 00:21:30,331
Figure it out how?

405
00:21:30,915 --> 00:21:32,708
The way I figure things out.

406
00:21:34,835 --> 00:21:35,920
That's your plan?

407
00:21:36,587 --> 00:21:37,880
Yeah, pretty much.

408
00:21:46,555 --> 00:21:47,598
60 seconds.

409
00:21:47,848 --> 00:21:50,351
Yeah, nothing like a little adrenaline
to get the mind fired up.

410
00:21:50,476 --> 00:21:51,852
I'm gonna need some quiet.

411
00:21:52,812 --> 00:21:55,022
- Cat alert, nine o'clock.
- Yeah, I'm not touching that.

412
00:21:58,609 --> 00:21:59,819
Time's running out.

413
00:22:01,362 --> 00:22:02,488
Have a little faith.

414
00:22:03,864 --> 00:22:05,574
- There. Got it.
- How'd you do that?

415
00:22:05,699 --> 00:22:07,827
- Well--
- Wylie texted it to him.

416
00:22:07,952 --> 00:22:09,954
- No, he didn't.
- When he hacked into the security system

417
00:22:10,079 --> 00:22:13,249
he saw Peterson punch in the code.
I saw you looking at your phone.

418
00:22:13,374 --> 00:22:15,084
Lisbon, you're killing the magic here.

419
00:22:15,209 --> 00:22:16,252
- Can we get moving?
- Sure.

420
00:22:16,377 --> 00:22:17,878
Remember, he's not gonna hide
the money

421
00:22:18,003 --> 00:22:19,255
in or under his mattress.

422
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:21,465
- Hidden compartments, false drawers.
- All over it.

423
00:24:30,386 --> 00:24:31,679
We got guns, no money.

424
00:24:32,096 --> 00:24:34,640
If the money's here someplace,
we can't find it.

425
00:24:35,474 --> 00:24:36,517
Jane?

426
00:24:36,642 --> 00:24:38,644
Candlesticks seem a little odd,
don't they?

427
00:24:38,769 --> 00:24:39,812
I don't understand.

428
00:24:39,937 --> 00:24:42,690
I mean, for a thin strip
of Texas leather like Peterson.

429
00:24:43,107 --> 00:24:44,984
Doesn't strike me
as the candlestick type.

430
00:24:45,150 --> 00:24:46,360
Maybe he hired a designer.

431
00:24:46,777 --> 00:24:47,820
Maybe.

432
00:24:53,158 --> 00:24:54,201
China.

433
00:25:03,335 --> 00:25:05,671
- Oh, this is a problem.
- Don't you know how to open a safe?

434
00:25:05,796 --> 00:25:08,590
Biometrics. We need
Peterson's thumbprint to open that.

435
00:25:08,757 --> 00:25:09,800
Well, what are we gonna do?

436
00:25:10,134 --> 00:25:12,845
- Okay, so we go to the backup plan.
- What's that?

437
00:25:13,095 --> 00:25:14,388
We have about 40 minutes left.

438
00:25:14,513 --> 00:25:16,849
If we can't get to Peterson's money,
we'll use our own money.

439
00:25:17,016 --> 00:25:18,058
What money?

440
00:25:18,642 --> 00:25:20,894
Uh, Wylie, I need you to get
into the evidence room

441
00:25:21,020 --> 00:25:22,479
and get us some cash.

442
00:25:23,564 --> 00:25:24,606
How much?

443
00:25:25,149 --> 00:25:26,984
At least a million dollars.
Two would be better.

444
00:25:27,109 --> 00:25:30,279
That's... big. I'm--
I'm not sure how I'd do that.

445
00:25:30,612 --> 00:25:32,948
Do your best.
Yeah, it's very important.

446
00:25:33,824 --> 00:25:35,326
I don't have clearance for that.

447
00:25:36,702 --> 00:25:37,828
Let me, uh...

448
00:25:38,412 --> 00:25:39,580
Let me think.

449
00:25:44,251 --> 00:25:45,419
I'll get back to you.

450
00:25:50,507 --> 00:25:51,550
Sir.

451
00:25:52,634 --> 00:25:53,677
What is it?

452
00:25:53,802 --> 00:25:55,512
You know how Jane said
he wouldn't need your help?

453
00:25:58,349 --> 00:25:59,683
He lied, didn't he?

454
00:26:04,104 --> 00:26:06,732
- What's up, Wylie?
- Vega, is Peterson still at Blatt's office?

455
00:26:06,857 --> 00:26:07,900
"Yeah. He just got there."

456
00:26:08,025 --> 00:26:09,401
Any chance he'll go to work
when he's done?

457
00:26:09,526 --> 00:26:11,987
I called his office. They said
he's not expected in today. Why?

458
00:26:12,780 --> 00:26:14,239
I'm going to San Antonio.

459
00:26:14,365 --> 00:26:16,158
"It's gonna take me
about 40 minutes to get there."

460
00:26:16,283 --> 00:26:18,786
If Peterson leaves in the next
half an hour, stall him, okay?

461
00:26:18,911 --> 00:26:21,330
Stall him? Uh, okay.

462
00:26:56,323 --> 00:26:57,491
Stall him.

463
00:26:58,158 --> 00:26:59,201
Stall him.

464
00:27:02,579 --> 00:27:04,248
Stall him.

465
00:27:12,923 --> 00:27:16,135
Oh, my God, I am so sorry!
I'll get you my insurance!

466
00:27:26,895 --> 00:27:28,063
Did you see that?

467
00:27:28,188 --> 00:27:29,731
Could've been worse.
You stop short?

468
00:27:29,940 --> 00:27:32,985
No, I didn't stop short!
She ran right into me!

469
00:27:33,110 --> 00:27:34,987
I'm so sorry! Are you okay?

470
00:27:35,154 --> 00:27:37,156
How do I know?
You ran right into me!

471
00:27:37,281 --> 00:27:39,241
You'll wanna fill out a report
for your insurance.

472
00:27:40,117 --> 00:27:42,327
Oh, for God's sake!

473
00:27:58,302 --> 00:27:59,720
- Is there a problem?
- I--

474
00:27:59,845 --> 00:28:01,305
I know I just got them renewed.

475
00:28:01,430 --> 00:28:03,307
I put them in here.
I took out the old ones.

476
00:28:03,432 --> 00:28:05,225
- Put the new ones in.
- Miss?

477
00:28:05,434 --> 00:28:07,728
I just feel so terrible about this.

478
00:28:07,936 --> 00:28:10,981
I know you!
You're one of Abbott's people.

479
00:28:13,484 --> 00:28:15,152
Agent Peterson?

480
00:28:15,277 --> 00:28:19,406
Oh, now I am really embarrassed.
I am so sorry, sir. Uh--

481
00:28:19,573 --> 00:28:22,576
The sun was in my eyes and I thought
you were a lot further away from me.

482
00:28:22,743 --> 00:28:24,620
Look, uh, just--
just forget about it.

483
00:28:24,786 --> 00:28:27,331
Oh, no, sir, we--
we could never do that.

484
00:28:27,498 --> 00:28:28,790
There is so much damage

485
00:28:28,957 --> 00:28:30,125
- to your car here.
- I'm not worried about it.

486
00:28:30,292 --> 00:28:32,127
We really should fill out
a report, though.

487
00:28:32,252 --> 00:28:34,671
- Your car really looks terrible.
- I'm in a hurry!

488
00:28:40,761 --> 00:28:42,304
"I tried to stop him,
but he wouldn't stay."

489
00:28:42,429 --> 00:28:44,848
- Which way's he heading?
- East. He's headed your way.

490
00:28:45,015 --> 00:28:46,308
"Well, how far are you?"

491
00:28:46,433 --> 00:28:48,060
About 15 minutes. How far is Wylie?

492
00:28:48,185 --> 00:28:49,353
About 15 minutes.

493
00:29:03,825 --> 00:29:05,285
I got here as fast as I could.

494
00:29:05,410 --> 00:29:06,453
Thank you.

495
00:29:10,541 --> 00:29:11,750
Good luck. Bye.

496
00:30:02,289 --> 00:30:05,000
Obviously the issue with protected
zones is colored by money

497
00:30:05,125 --> 00:30:07,044
- but we're gonna have to--
- Mrs. Abbott.

498
00:30:08,087 --> 00:30:09,129
Dennis?

499
00:30:09,421 --> 00:30:10,965
Linda, I'll catch up with you later.

500
00:30:11,257 --> 00:30:12,758
Oh... Baby.

501
00:30:15,052 --> 00:30:17,846
- What are you doing in D.C.?
- Well, I wanted to see you.

502
00:30:19,139 --> 00:30:20,391
And I know you're scared.

503
00:30:20,849 --> 00:30:23,269
- More like terrified.
- Well, don't be.

504
00:30:24,311 --> 00:30:27,106
I trust Jane.
And he hasn't let me down so far.

505
00:30:27,231 --> 00:30:28,691
Dennis, we need a miracle.

506
00:30:28,857 --> 00:30:31,569
Listen. You will be confirmed.

507
00:30:33,279 --> 00:30:34,655
And we'll be together.

508
00:30:35,906 --> 00:30:38,158
I have to go.
The hearing's about to start.

509
00:30:38,284 --> 00:30:41,495
Okay, I'll walk you in.
I have something to do inside anyway.

510
00:30:48,252 --> 00:30:49,295
Abbott.

511
00:30:49,670 --> 00:30:52,840
- Funny meeting you here.
- Well, I could say the same thing, Bill.

512
00:30:52,965 --> 00:30:54,133
I guess you haven't heard.

513
00:30:54,258 --> 00:30:57,011
It's not official yet,
but I'm gonna be a witness

514
00:30:57,177 --> 00:30:58,929
at your wife's hearing.

515
00:30:59,054 --> 00:31:00,764
Ah. Well, who did that?

516
00:31:01,307 --> 00:31:02,433
Blatt.

517
00:31:03,183 --> 00:31:04,685
Representative Jules Blatt

518
00:31:04,810 --> 00:31:06,228
put your name on the list
to be a witness?

519
00:31:06,353 --> 00:31:08,939
I have evidence he thinks
people should hear about.

520
00:31:09,064 --> 00:31:10,274
I think you know what it is.

521
00:31:10,399 --> 00:31:13,319
Evidence, huh?
Evidence that you brought him?

522
00:31:13,569 --> 00:31:15,738
What is this, Abbott?
What are you doing?

523
00:31:15,988 --> 00:31:17,865
You know, maybe I should talk to Blatt.

524
00:31:18,282 --> 00:31:19,867
You see, I have evidence, too.

525
00:31:20,326 --> 00:31:21,577
Yeah. Take a look.

526
00:31:22,119 --> 00:31:23,203
Now that's nice.

527
00:31:24,204 --> 00:31:27,750
Okay, that's the best one right there.

528
00:31:28,751 --> 00:31:30,085
What, Bill?

529
00:31:33,922 --> 00:31:35,799
Wait, Bill?

530
00:31:42,514 --> 00:31:44,141
Seems we both have secrets.

531
00:31:44,767 --> 00:31:46,060
Cute kitty, by the way.

532
00:31:56,278 --> 00:31:58,489
- Put your hands up.
- You think I'm wearing a wire?

533
00:31:58,614 --> 00:31:59,657
Up!

534
00:32:00,991 --> 00:32:02,201
Come on, Bill!

535
00:32:02,368 --> 00:32:05,829
This is just a conversation
between two old friends, right?

536
00:32:06,705 --> 00:32:08,248
You broke into my home.

537
00:32:08,374 --> 00:32:11,377
I have people that will account
for every minute of my time.

538
00:32:11,502 --> 00:32:14,338
If it wasn't you, then it was Jane
or one of those other idiots.

539
00:32:14,463 --> 00:32:16,840
That's a crime, not to mention
illegal search.

540
00:32:17,216 --> 00:32:20,219
Well, that'll be for the D.A. to sort out.

541
00:32:20,761 --> 00:32:24,431
But it will get the attention
of the Inspector General.

542
00:32:24,765 --> 00:32:26,850
And there will be an internal investigation

543
00:32:26,975 --> 00:32:28,769
and they'll look into all of your old busts

544
00:32:28,894 --> 00:32:32,272
and realize that you have been
a thief for 20 years.

545
00:32:35,693 --> 00:32:37,236
I have you, Bill.

546
00:32:38,404 --> 00:32:40,656
I have you by the short and curlies.

547
00:32:42,783 --> 00:32:43,826
Okay.

548
00:32:46,328 --> 00:32:48,247
Okay. Fine.

549
00:32:49,998 --> 00:32:51,291
Take half.

550
00:32:51,458 --> 00:32:53,752
We both walk away. This is over.

551
00:32:53,877 --> 00:32:55,421
Wow. Half.

552
00:32:56,171 --> 00:32:59,007
- How much is half?
- About 800.

553
00:32:59,299 --> 00:33:03,470
So you're offering me $800,000
of the cash that you've been skimming?

554
00:33:03,595 --> 00:33:06,682
- You're not getting a dime more.
- I don't want any more.

555
00:33:06,807 --> 00:33:08,600
Hello again, Agent Peterson.

556
00:33:12,271 --> 00:33:13,897
I have all I need.

557
00:33:23,490 --> 00:33:25,284
It doesn't matter what you do to me.

558
00:33:25,826 --> 00:33:27,661
- It can't help.
- Why not?

559
00:33:27,786 --> 00:33:31,582
Blatt has the bullet.
The bullet you shot into that Zeta's head.

560
00:33:32,583 --> 00:33:33,959
Bullet.

561
00:33:34,293 --> 00:33:36,044
Was that the evidence
you were talking about?

562
00:33:36,170 --> 00:33:38,839
That's right. And when Blatt
gets done with it

563
00:33:39,423 --> 00:33:42,259
your wife can kiss
her new job goodbye.

564
00:33:42,676 --> 00:33:44,720
And you're going to prison, my friend.

565
00:33:44,845 --> 00:33:46,680
- Abbott, Peterson, Vega.
- Hey.

566
00:33:46,889 --> 00:33:47,931
Uh, good to see you all.

567
00:33:48,056 --> 00:33:52,936
All me to introduce Congressman Blatt's
legislative director, Missy, um...

568
00:33:53,562 --> 00:33:55,856
- Uh... I'm sorry. What is it?
- Uh, Brammer.

569
00:33:56,774 --> 00:33:58,108
Sorry. Missy Brammer.

570
00:33:58,233 --> 00:34:00,027
Uh, you said you had a constituent
who had to see me?

571
00:34:00,152 --> 00:34:02,571
Yes, Miss Brammer.
This is Bill Peterson.

572
00:34:02,696 --> 00:34:06,492
He is one of the Congressman's
most enthusiastic supporters.

573
00:34:06,617 --> 00:34:09,453
- Oh, how do you do?
- Yeah, wait, wait... no.

574
00:34:10,120 --> 00:34:11,371
This isn't right.

575
00:34:11,538 --> 00:34:13,457
- I'm sorry?
- There's been a mistake.

576
00:34:13,582 --> 00:34:17,044
I met Missy Brammer
two days ago in San Antonio.

577
00:34:17,169 --> 00:34:20,756
Well, that's impossible, because
Miss Brammer's been here in D.C.

578
00:34:20,881 --> 00:34:23,133
all week
preparing for the hearings.

579
00:34:23,258 --> 00:34:25,135
Uh, which I'm gonna be late for
if I don't hurry.

580
00:34:25,260 --> 00:34:26,345
- Okay.
- Nice to meet you all.

581
00:34:26,845 --> 00:34:27,888
Mr. Peterson.

582
00:34:29,097 --> 00:34:30,265
Hey. Wait--

583
00:34:32,810 --> 00:34:34,269
No, no, no.

584
00:34:34,394 --> 00:34:37,105
She returned my call from Blatt's office.

585
00:34:37,231 --> 00:34:39,608
"Hi. My cell phone just died.
Could I use one of yours?"

586
00:34:39,733 --> 00:34:41,401
"Sure. Missy's outta town.
You can use hers."

587
00:34:42,319 --> 00:34:44,029
- "Thank you very much."
- "You're welcome."

588
00:34:46,657 --> 00:34:48,742
- "Bill Peterson."
- "Hello, Mr. Peterson."

589
00:34:50,160 --> 00:34:51,537
"This is Missy Brammer."

590
00:34:52,287 --> 00:34:54,706
"I'm Congressman Blatt's
legislative director."

591
00:34:54,873 --> 00:34:57,960
I met her! In Blatt's office.

592
00:34:58,335 --> 00:35:02,297
"Sorry, everybody. Pest control.
We need the office for 20 minutes."

593
00:35:02,422 --> 00:35:05,133
"All right. Everybody out.
They gotta spray."

594
00:35:29,992 --> 00:35:32,452
- "Hey. Hello."
- "Come in."

595
00:35:36,290 --> 00:35:38,625
- "I got something for you."
- "Thank you."

596
00:35:41,253 --> 00:35:42,296
"Here we go."

597
00:35:50,762 --> 00:35:51,805
Bill?

598
00:35:52,055 --> 00:35:54,725
I can't tell you how much
it means to me to say this.

599
00:35:55,851 --> 00:35:57,311
You're under arrest.

600
00:36:00,506 --> 00:36:02,216
Wonderful to see the government
at work.

601
00:36:14,119 --> 00:36:17,580
- Guys, let me drive you to the airport.
- Nah, you've done enough already.

602
00:36:17,872 --> 00:36:20,500
- Well, thank you for everything.
- Aw...

603
00:36:22,043 --> 00:36:24,129
You know what?
That was some good, clean fun.

604
00:36:24,254 --> 00:36:26,464
- Like old times.
- Just like old times.

605
00:36:27,173 --> 00:36:28,716
Pete, hold the door a sec.

606
00:36:29,509 --> 00:36:32,470
He tells me you got a sweetheart.
That little brunette?

607
00:36:32,804 --> 00:36:33,847
That is true.

608
00:36:34,264 --> 00:36:35,765
Don't screw it up, Patrick.

609
00:36:36,349 --> 00:36:39,060
- I'll try not to.
- Life's too short.

610
00:36:44,149 --> 00:36:45,191
See ya.

611
00:36:45,316 --> 00:36:46,484
- Bye.
- Come on...

612
00:36:59,414 --> 00:37:01,624
"Careful there. Now don't spill any."

613
00:37:01,749 --> 00:37:03,209
- Um...
- Plastic cups?

614
00:37:04,711 --> 00:37:07,172
Okay. A toast.

615
00:37:08,131 --> 00:37:09,382
To friends.

616
00:37:09,716 --> 00:37:12,760
Thank you for... all...

617
00:37:14,429 --> 00:37:17,432
- We are forever in your debt.
- Yes, yes.

618
00:37:17,557 --> 00:37:19,893
We are forever in your debt.
I-I make horrible speeches.

619
00:37:20,018 --> 00:37:21,686
- I'm so sorry.
- Cheers. Cheers.

620
00:37:21,853 --> 00:37:24,230
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

621
00:37:26,858 --> 00:37:29,569
- Let's go check out that waffle truck.
- Oh, definitely.

622
00:37:34,991 --> 00:37:37,410
So how fast were you going
when you drove to San Antonio?

623
00:37:37,535 --> 00:37:40,580
Like a hundred million miles an hour.
Roughly.

624
00:37:40,705 --> 00:37:42,916
How fast were you going when you
rammed the back of Peterson's car?

625
00:37:43,082 --> 00:37:46,044
- Like 3 miles an hour.
- Still, I bet it was pretty cool.

626
00:37:46,211 --> 00:37:48,296
It was. It was really cool.

627
00:37:51,424 --> 00:37:53,092
You know, I don't want a waffle.
You wanna dance?

628
00:37:55,220 --> 00:37:57,430
No. No, I'm--
I'm a terrible dancer.

629
00:37:57,555 --> 00:38:00,600
Oh, come on.
What do you have to lose, Wylie?

630
00:38:00,767 --> 00:38:02,310
Come on. Let's go.

631
00:38:02,435 --> 00:38:03,645
Okay, but you can't laugh.

632
00:38:04,103 --> 00:38:05,271
No promises.

633
00:38:06,314 --> 00:38:09,067
Well, since everything
is cleared up with Lena

634
00:38:09,192 --> 00:38:12,403
I'll be moving in a couple of months
work from the D.C. office.

635
00:38:12,529 --> 00:38:13,821
So you know what that means.

636
00:38:14,906 --> 00:38:15,990
It's you.

637
00:38:17,408 --> 00:38:19,369
- What is?
- The unit.

638
00:38:20,370 --> 00:38:21,412
It's yours.

639
00:38:22,247 --> 00:38:23,581
You'll be in charge.

640
00:38:23,915 --> 00:38:26,793
I talked to the regional office
I talked to the assistant director

641
00:38:26,918 --> 00:38:28,920
and everyone agrees.

642
00:38:30,296 --> 00:38:31,673
You've earned it, Kimball.

643
00:38:32,173 --> 00:38:34,551
And I think you're gonna do
a great job at it.

644
00:38:41,975 --> 00:38:43,351
Thank you, sir.

645
00:38:43,893 --> 00:38:45,770
No. Thank you.

646
00:38:46,521 --> 00:38:49,524
Well, since that's settled, I think
I am going to dance

647
00:38:49,649 --> 00:38:54,571
with the next undersecretary
of the department of commerce.

648
00:38:54,862 --> 00:38:57,031
Get on the dance floor, baby.
Come on.

649
00:39:00,243 --> 00:39:02,328
- Congratulations, Cho.
- Thank you.

650
00:39:02,579 --> 00:39:03,746
You deserve it.

651
00:39:07,500 --> 00:39:09,127
I'm gonna go get another taco.

652
00:39:09,502 --> 00:39:10,670
Okay.

653
00:39:16,050 --> 00:39:19,053
You know, we never finished
our conversation.

654
00:39:19,262 --> 00:39:20,555
No, it got busy.

655
00:39:21,556 --> 00:39:24,642
- Life throws you curve balls.
- Are we talking about baseball now?

656
00:39:24,767 --> 00:39:27,478
I'm serious. I want us
to be strong together.

657
00:39:27,604 --> 00:39:28,896
We are, aren't we?

658
00:39:30,481 --> 00:39:33,192
I love you, and I also love
what I do.

659
00:39:33,651 --> 00:39:35,612
You can't be jealous of that.

660
00:39:35,778 --> 00:39:37,113
Oh, it's not that.

661
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:39,240
What is it?

662
00:39:45,079 --> 00:39:46,122
What?

663
00:39:48,583 --> 00:39:50,460
I don't wanna lose you.

664
00:39:51,419 --> 00:39:53,880
I don't know how I would react.

665
00:39:56,049 --> 00:39:58,343
- You're not gonna lose me.
- You don't know that.

666
00:39:58,468 --> 00:40:00,303
Nobody knows what's gonna happen.

667
00:40:00,803 --> 00:40:02,889
You could die tomorrow.
I could.

668
00:40:03,681 --> 00:40:06,643
We need to focus on
what's going on right now.

669
00:40:07,477 --> 00:40:09,270
Right here. It's good.

670
00:40:09,395 --> 00:40:11,481
It is very, very good.

671
00:40:13,566 --> 00:40:14,609
Yeah.

672
00:40:16,694 --> 00:40:17,737
Okay.

673
00:40:17,862 --> 00:40:19,447
- Are you sure?
- Yeah.

674
00:40:22,700 --> 00:40:24,118
Do you wanna dance?

675
00:40:24,243 --> 00:40:26,079
- Here? In front of everyone?
- Sure.

676
00:40:27,455 --> 00:40:29,499
- Okay, one dance.
- Two.

677
00:40:30,708 --> 00:40:33,044
Everything is a negotiation
with you.

678
00:40:36,760 --> 00:40:37,803
Come on.

